/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A planar guide confines light only across its thickness.
   - Names vary between books: many use 'channel waveguide' for any guide that confines in two directions, strips and ridges included, and others reserve it for the buried core.
   - The thickness direction is a slab, so the slab V of the mode-theory chapter applies, with the same full-thickness convention:
   - t is the core thickness and λ the vacuum wavelength in the same unit; √(n₁² − n₂²) is the NA of the index step, here a measure of contrast rather than a launch angle.
   - The effective index method handles the width.
   - Because neff,1 is below n₁, the lateral contrast is weaker than the material contrast, so a guide is often much wider than it is thick before a second lateral mode appears.
   - Photonic integrated circuits combine guides with Y-branch splitters, directional couplers, ring resonators, arrayed waveguide gratings and modulators on one chip.
*/
